# OPT-IN: exclude everything, then re-include only what a Dockerfile actually reads.
#
# The build context is the WORKSPACE ROOT — a consequence of the migration's image change,
# where the Dockerfiles COPY prebuilt output instead of pulling published tarballs. In a
# single-component repository the context is small by accident; here it is 1.9 GB of root
# node_modules plus a per-package node_modules for all 33 members, every dist tree, .nx, and
# a 125 MB .git.
#
# The first version of this file was opt-out — exclude the known-bad, keep the rest — and it
# failed the way opt-out always eventually does: `**/node_modules` also matched
# `.deploy/build-task-consumer/node_modules`, which the image genuinely needs, and the build
# died on a COPY source that the guard step had just confirmed was present on disk. The
# failure mode of opt-out is a silent, surprising EXCLUSION.
#
# Opt-in inverts that. Its failure mode is a missing path, which is loud, immediate, and names
# itself — and the list below is then a precise, reviewable statement of what each image is
# allowed to see. Adding a COPY to a Dockerfile without adding it here fails at once rather
# than shipping something unintended.
#
# Keep this list in step with the COPY lines in:
#   frontend/ui/Dockerfile
#   frontend/build-task-consumer/Dockerfile

# Everything, at every depth.
**

# --- frontend/ui -----------------------------------------------------------------------
!frontend/ui/nginx/**
!frontend/ui/packages/agents/dist/**
!frontend/ui/packages/portal/dist/**

# --- frontend/build-task-consumer ------------------------------------------------------
!frontend/build-task-consumer/dist/**
# Produced on the host by `pnpm deploy --legacy --prod` before the build. It is gitignored,
# which does not matter — docker does not read .gitignore — but it does have to be named here.
!.deploy/**

# …except pnpm's own bookkeeping, which is the reason this image's largest layer could never
# be cached. Measured: two `pnpm deploy` runs to the SAME absolute path, in the SAME job,
# produce 16316 identical files, 1085 identical symlink targets, identical modes and sizes —
# and differ in exactly ONE file, `node_modules/.modules.yaml`. That is enough to change the
# cache key of `COPY .deploy/build-task-consumer/node_modules`, so buildx rebuilt and
# re-exported the whole 16316-file layer on every single build and never once hit it.
#
# It is pnpm's record of how node_modules was assembled — store location, layout version,
# what was skipped, when it was last pruned. Nothing reads it at runtime; the application
# needs the packages, not the note about how they got there. Excluding it changes what the
# image receives, not what the deploy tree contains.
#
# Ordering matters: this must come AFTER the re-include above, because docker applies
# patterns in order and the last match wins.
.deploy/*/node_modules/.modules.yaml

# Nothing else. No sources, no node_modules outside the deploy tree, no .git, no .nx, no
# tests, no docs. If a build needs something, it goes above with the reason.
